Phase 6, fifth slice: a second, deeper layer of the same bug evidence 51
fixed - found only by actually running the real, downloaded model against
the real, pinned server, not by reading documentation more carefully.

## 1. What evidence 51 got right, and what it still got wrong

Evidence 51 fixed the shape (flat `response_format.schema`, not OpenAI's
nested `response_format.json_schema.schema`) by reading llama.cpp's
`tools/server/README.md` at the exact pinned commit (`5f436dddb...`,
build b10948). That fix was necessary and correct. It was not sufficient:
the same README documents two valid `Type` values for schema-constrained
output, `"json_object"` and `"json_schema"`, and evidence 51 picked
`"json_schema"` as the more semantically apt-sounding name.

## 2. What a live smoke test found

Before running the real corpus against the real downloaded model
(unsloth/Qwen3-4B-Instruct-2507-GGUF, Q4_K_M, SHA-256 verified against
Hugging Face's own LFS `oid` before use; llama.cpp b10948 Windows CPU
build, SHA-256 verified against the GitHub release's own recorded digest)
on the second test machine, a direct request was sent to the running
server with `"type": "json_schema"` and a required three-field schema
(`summary`, `evidence_handles` as a closed two-value enum, `confidence`).
The model answered with `{"event": "eth0 link status changed from down
to up"}` - a completely different, unconstrained shape, no error of any
kind from the server. The exact same request with `"type": "json_object"`
and the identical flat `schema` field produced `{"summary": "eth0 started
down, then came up.", "evidence_handles": ["E1","E2"], "confidence": 0}` -
every required field present, the enum constraint honored exactly.

This is the same class of finding as evidence 51 (a request shape that
does not error but silently constrains nothing), one layer deeper: not a
structural mismatch this time, but a `Type` value the README lists as
valid that this specific build does not actually honor in practice for
constraint purposes, while its sibling value does. Whether this is a
version-specific gap between documentation and implementation, or some
other build-specific quirk, was not investigated further - the empirical
result against the real, pinned binary is what this project ships
against, not a theory about why the documentation and the binary
disagree.

## 3. The fix

`responseFormat.Type` value sent by `chatComplete` changed from
`"json_schema"` to `"json_object"` (the schema itself is unchanged - still
the flat, un-nested shape evidence 51 fixed). The doc comment on
`responseFormat` is rewritten to state plainly that empirically-verified
behavior of the real binary was preferred over what its own documentation
states, and to point at this evidence log and the raw smoke-test request/
response pairs.

## 4. Full regression

    $ go build ./...                               clean
    $ go vet ./...                                  clean
    $ CGO_ENABLED=0 go test ./...                   every package passes

## 5. Proven, not just asserted

`TestChatCompleteSendsTheEmpiricallyVerifiedTypeValue` (new): marshals a
real `chatCompletionRequest` built the same way `chatComplete` builds one
and asserts the wire value is exactly `"type":"json_object"` - pinning
the specific, non-obvious, documentation-contradicting value a future
edit could otherwise "correct" back to the more official-sounding
`"json_schema"` without realizing that value silently does nothing
against the real server. The two pre-existing response-format tests
(`TestChatCompletionRequestSendsThePerCaseSchema`,
`TestResponseFormatMatchesLlamaServersOwnShapeNotOpenAIs`) were updated
to construct their fixtures with `"json_object"` too, so nothing in the
test suite continues to exercise the value now known not to work.

## 6. Why this matters beyond one flag value

This is the second time in two slices that a request-shape assumption
built and unit-tested entirely against Go's own type system turned out
to not do what was intended against the real target - first a structural
mismatch (evidence 51), now a documented-but-non-functional value
(this log). Both were caught before a single real benchmark case ran
against them, specifically because this session insisted on a live smoke
test against the actual downloaded model before trusting either fix, not
because either bug was visible from reading the Go code alone. The
benchmark run against the `test` split proceeds only now, using the
build proven correct by this smoke test, not the one that merely
compiled and passed its own unit tests.
